\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Chile Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Hasn't Seen Need To End  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 18:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 12:21 (Dow Jones) Despite the peso sliding 13% against the dollar in September and continuing its tumble further into October, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't seen a need to end its daily dollar purchases ahead of schedule, a bank governor said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is continously monitoring the peso and if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't decided to end the program early, it's because we haven't considered it necessary," Sebastian Claro, the youngest of the five governors said. (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 (Dow Jones) Brazilian steelmakers won't succeed with reported plans to increase domestic steel prices by 10% for November/December bookings, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. According to Steel Business Briefing report, steel executives believe that the recent strength of the US dollar should support price hikes in Brazil. However, domestic market price increases still unlikely in the current scenario of decelerating demand, declining global steel prices and high economic uncertainties, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:13 (Dow Jones) DJIA hasn't fallen 20% below its May 2 intraday peak (12876), but that doesn't mean a bear trend hasn't been confirmed. The century-old Dow Theory states both the DJIA and the Dow Jones Transportation Average needed to both hit fresh lows (or highs) to signal a bear (or bull) market. The shorter the length of time between the new lows, the stronger the confirmation. DJIA closed yesterday at a low for the year, the same day the Dow transports did the same. DJIA down 63 at 10592, or about 18% below its May 2 high. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:10 (Dow Jones) When oil prices were trading well above $100/barrel in 2008, Saudi Arabia increased production, aiming to push prices below $80 and help the world economy as it was falling into recession. Then, Saudi Arabia's break-even level was around $60. But now, a major fiscal expansion in response to the Arab Spring has moved that up to around $92/barrel for Brent, estimates \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . As such, that could prompt output cuts in order to defend that level. Prices have been heading lower the past five months, and slumped since early September, with Brent briefly falling below $100 Tuesday. (isabel.ordonez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:07 (Dow Jones) Deutsche Boerse (DB1.XE) chief executive \uc2 Reto Francioni\uc2  tells a Baruch College audience that there will "soon be a very limited number of very global exchanges," and combined Deutsche Boerse-\uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  (NYX) will be "the most international exchange organization there is." He defends global exchanges as safe for investors and more transparent. CEO gives no reaction to Reuters report that EU regulators are set to object to the deal formally, nor does he discuss potential regulatory roadblocks, declining comment to Dow Jones Newswires on event's sidelines. (brendan.conway@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 (Dow Jones) The 2-year swap spread, a main gauge of credit risks, keeps blowing out and now trades at the widest level since June 2010. Growing worries about counterparty risks -- especially on banks exposed to Greek government bonds, such as \uc2 Dexia\uc2 , could spell more problems for euro-zone banks, sending their funding costs even higher. Shares of big US banks, such as \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , have fallen significantly in recent sessions and bank bonds have taken a beating. Taking note of possible fallout from euro-zone crisis, Bernanke says Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to give US banks liquidity if European woes hit. The 2-yr US swap spread recently widens by about 2bps to 37.75bps. The spread has jumped from the recent low of 14bps in April.(min.zeng@downones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:54 (Dow Jones) Shares of builder Hovnanian (HOV) down 5.6% at $1.01, earlier as low as $1.00. To be sure, most builders have seen their share prices fall after the key spring selling season fizzled and broader economic fears mount. "Over the last 2 months the stocks have fallen off a cliff as investors are now worried about a 'double dip' recession," writes the Housing Research Center. "The builders just can't seem to catch a break." HOV is down 75% YTD, while Beazer (BZH) has plunged 73%. \uc2 NVR\uc2 's relatively modest 17% decline is best performer for the battered pack. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 (Dow Jones) CME aims by 2012 to be much busier handling trades in complex derivatives contracts, planning services for clearing cash-settled forward contracts in G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more non-deliverable forwards in "major emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it tells investors at its analyst day. The company already handles some NDFs in Latin America, but is seeking a bigger chunk of off-exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives as an adjunct to its industry-leading volume in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 (Dow Jones) One of the more prominent legacies of QE2 was the dramatic surge of gold, which has its own perceived dual mandate of being a safe-haven metal and harbinger of inflation expectations. Yet in the wake of Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's testimony, in which he initially appeared to suggest a QE3 only to walk it back, the precious metal is tumbling more than 2% on the session to $1,619.20 as the dollar pares its earlier losses. That may reflect waning expectations of more easing, and may also be a sign that investors are more comfortable holding dollars in a deeply unsettled environment. EUR/USD at 1.3264, still up 0.67% on the day. USD/CHF rises 0.31% to trade around 0.9245 while USD/JPY gains 0.33% to 76.88. (javier.david@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:44 (Dow Jones) A new analyst takes charge of covering \uc2 American Express\uc2  (AXP) at Jefferies, and he rates the credit-card company at hold, not buy as the investment bank previously had AXP, with the price target changed to $50 from $53. "We expect credit normalization to begin in early 2012, driving credit losses and provision expense higher. Credit normalization combined with the loss of $880M in annual legal payments from Visa (V) and \uc2 MasterCard\uc2  (MA) will likely inhibit meaningful earnings growth in 2012." Still, Jefferies says, "AXP's affluent customer base should help to keep this stock defensively positioned despite the slow recovery of the global economy." AXP falls 4.5% to $41.53, putting the stock into the red for the year. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:42 (Dow Jones) It's not unusual for the S&P 500 to bounce sharply after crossing the bear-market threshold, as it's done today. The last time a downtrend for the index first got 20% below the market's recent high intraday was March 17, 2008. It fell as much as 2.4% that day but finished off just 0.9%. It took another 3 1/2 months to again break that 20% level and another week before closing below it. That was a mere two months before the financial crisis hit its screaming pitch and sent stocks plunging. The S&P 500 is now down 0.9% at 1089.5, off its low of 1074.5. The bear-market thresholds are 1096.46 (intraday basis) and 1090.89 (closing basis). (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:40 (Dow Jones) US dollar's recent strengthening is bad news for retailers that do a lot of business overseas, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says in cutting 2011-2013 earnings estimates for \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT), \uc2 Costco\uc2  (COST) and PVH. "Virtually every \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impacting our coverage has weakened, with the exception of the yen, over the past two months," firm says. Goldman lifts estimates for Tiffany (TIF), which does a lot of business in Japan, saying yen's strength more than offsets the weaker euro and UK pound. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-04-11 1221ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020111004e7a4000hk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
