\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Pinera: Can't Meet All Demands Of Protesters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Wisnefski and Eduardo Kaplan   \par \uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2011\par 22:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Chilean President Sebastian Pinera, facing protests at home by students seeking higher subsidies among other reforms, said Thursday he sympathizes with the demands but that the government needs to maintain fiscal discipline amid a deteriorating global economic scenario.\par \par \uc2 "We can accept some of the conditions but not all the conditions," Pinera said in an interview, noting that student demands for a free college education and the elimination of for-profit schools aren't practical.\par \par \uc2 "We could spend all the money and everyone would be happy for the day, but then what would happen?," Pinera said, referring to funds the government has accumulated thanks to the recovery of Chilean economic growth in recent years.\par \par \uc2 "We don't want to face the problems Greece and Portugal are facing now, " Pinera said.\par \par \uc2 Students, who have staged periodic protests over the past four months seeking higher-quality and more-affordable education, took to the streets again Thursday in Chile, with tens of thousands of protesters in the capital Santiago, according to local media reports.\par \par \uc2 Polls show that the protests have won broad sympathy among older Chileans frustrated by income inequalities in a country long seen as a model of economic stability in Latin America. Unable to allay the students since the protests started in May, Pinera has seen his approval rating plunge over the past several months from the peak of popularity he enjoyed following the daring rescue of trapped miners a year ago. While educational reform was already a priority for his administration, students want quicker and more-aggressive action.\par \par \uc2 The government in early July committed $4 billion beyond what had been budgeted for education, money that Pinera said would come from the extra revenue that was generated thanks to a recovery in growth since the 2008 financial crisis and increased revenues from rising copper prices. Pinera has pledged to improve the quality of education at all levels and to provide free education for all middle and low income students.\par \par \uc2 Pinera said he's hopeful a compromise will be reached, but urged those seeking reforms to be pragmatic. "People think we can live as if we were a rich country - we're not," Pinera said. "I am concerned that some people think we have already reached the summit. We are halfway down the path to full development."\par \par \uc2 Pinera, who took office in March 2010 just after the country was devastated by an earthquake and tsunami, said he's "very concerned" about deteriorating global economic conditions. Chile is a very open economy with numerous free trade agreements, which makes it vulnerable to the global turmoil, Pinera said.\par \par \uc2 Pinera's hopes for returning the country to economic growth rates of 6% and higher necessary to broadly improve living standards may suffer as a result. His government has already tempered its economic expansion forecasts for this year and next, and Pinera said Europe's process to get over its sovereign debt crisis "will be very slow and it will take a lot of time, and it will face a lot of risks."\par \par \uc2 Fears about the prospects for Europe and the global economy have caused the price of copper, Chile's main export, to fall to its lowest level in a year. The metal closed trading Thursday in New York at just under $3.50 a pound, down from a high for the year of $4.63 in February. Despite the decline, copper prices remain above the assumptions in the Chilean budget of $3.24 in 2011 and $3.02 in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Along with the decline in the price of copper, the Chilean peso has fallen to its weakest point against the dollar since July 2010, ending Thursday at CLP521. The peso has now weakened 12% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the start of the month.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the start of the year to stem the pace of appreciation of the peso, the weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be seen as a blessing for the export-reliant economy. But the swiftness of the recent reversal, which reflects heightened risk-aversion among global investors, could be cause for concern.\par \par \uc2 Pinera downplayed such concerns, noting that Chile views its peso against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not just the dollar, and against the basket, the peso has been "more stable". He also criticized the so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars of the past year, in which many countries, including Chile, have sought to stabilize their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect local industry.\par \par \uc2 "I think \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars and protectionist wars are the worst possible answer to this situation and any situation," Pinera said. However, "One of the problems that we have in the world right now is that some countries are not allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float as they should."\par \par \uc2 By Stephen Wisnefski and Eduardo Kaplan, (212) 416-2191; stephen.wisnefski@dowjones.com [ 22-09-11 2004GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110922e79m001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
