\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Peso Drops 1.6% As Copper Prices Continue To Tumble\b0\par\par\uc2 238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2011\par 13:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso weakened sharply against the dollar early Wednesday, to its weakest level since early January, as international copper prices continued to tumble on fears about anemic global growth and Europe's debt troubles.\par \par \uc2 The peso was recently trading 2.0% weaker at CLP499.35 to the dollar, versus Tuesday's close of CLP489.55.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 Spot copper prices in London fell 2.1% to an annual low of $3.72898 a pound, according to Chile's state copper commission Cochilco.\par \par \uc2 The peso is trading at levels not seen since the weeks after \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  implemented a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program at the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 "Fears of a further deterioration in the euro-zone, with rumors of a possible default of Greek sovereign debt and fears that the world economy could enter in a new recession," have weakened the peso, said investment bank Celfin Capital.\par \par \uc2 (Peso quote provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 21-09-11 1220GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110921e79l000uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
