\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ IMF Sees Chilean Peso In Line With Fundamentals\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 24 Aug 2011 15:03 EDT *DJ  IMF Sees No Need To Extend \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Currency Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 24 Aug 2011 15:07 EDT DJ  IMF: \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  Government Should Cut Spending Further\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean government needs to further reduce spending to ensure macroeconomic stability, the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Chile increased spending in 2009 to combat a recession stemming from the global financial crisis and has since reined in spending to contain inflationary and exchange-rate pressures.\par \par \uc2 "In the near term, greater fiscal restraint would help guard against upside inflation risks and improve the policy mix, reducing the risk of a surge in capital inflows and easing pressures on the exchange rate," the IMF said after completing the so-called Article IV annual consultation.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the countercyclical spending, the country in 2009 posted a record-high fiscal deficit of 4.5% of gross domestic product. In 2010, despite reconstruction efforts following an 8.8-magnitude earthquake and tsunami, the fiscal deficit fell to 0.4% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry expects the country to return to a balanced budget in 2014 and has taken steps to simplify the structural surplus rule in place since 2000, which has been credited with contributing to the country's economic stability.\par \par \uc2 The IMF praised these changes to the structural surplus rule, but warned the country needs to return to a fiscal surplus in the medium-term.\par \par \uc2 Regarding the peso, which has been trading at near three-year highs against the dollar despite a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, the IMF found the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trading in line with its fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 It noted it sees no need to extend the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program beyond its December end date.\par \par \uc2 Local exporters have been clamoring for additional government or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their products lose competitiveness abroad.\par \par \uc2 The relatively strong peso, in relation to the dollar, however, has helped keep import prices low.\par \par \uc2 While the IMF concurred with local expectations that GDP will grow 6.5% on the back of strong domestic demand, it noted the country's biggest challenge remains boosting productivity.\par \par \uc2 "Government initiatives to reduce the cost of doing business, increase competition, support research an development, and reform the corporate bankruptcy code are welcome," the IMF said.\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 24, 2011 15:07 ET (19:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110824e78o006df\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
