\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End Lower As Commodities-Related Companies Slump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 21:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended lower Monday, as heavy-weighted commodities-related shares slumped on the back of ongoing fears about the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended 0.5% lower at 4110.89 points, while market volume fell to 56.79 billion Chilean pesos ($121.5 million), compared with Friday's closing volume of CLP65.79 billion.\par \par \uc2 Commodities-related companies' shares are especially sensitive to expectations for global growth, which impact demand forecasts for their products.\par \par \uc2 Fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) fell 3.3% to CLP7,120.00 and paper and pulp producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) slipped 1.4% to CLP19,251.00.\par \par \uc2 The companies have a combined 16.1% weighting on the Ipsa.\par \par \uc2 "There's been a lot of talk about slowing global growth and if that comes to pass, the first companies that will be impacted are [commodities-related] companies," said Cristina Acle, studies head at brokerage Corpbanca Corredores de Bolsa\par \par \uc2 Additionally, power generator \uc2 Colbun (COLBUN.SN) \uc2 dropped 1.7% to CLP117.00 after it posted, late last week, a second-quarter net loss of $16.1 million, versus a $55.5 million gain in the year-earlier period, as reduced rainfall curtailed its hydroelectric power generation and forced it to use more expensive thermal generation.\par \par \uc2 Rising, however, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) gained 0.4% to CLP11,960.00 as participants expect Chile's TDLC antitrust court to rule on LAN's proposed merger with Brazil's TAM (TAMM4.BR) before month-end. Two out of three regulators in Brazil have already given the merger their thumbs up.\par \par \uc2 Also, home-durables manufacturing company Compania Tecno Industrial's (CTI.SN) shares jumped 11.8% to CLP39.10 after Swedish home appliance maker \uc2 Electrolux AB\uc2  (ELUX-B.SK) said it would buy a controlling stake in CTI and its subsidiaries. \uc2 Electrolux\uc2  will buy the controlling stake from Chilean industrial engineering conglomerate \uc2 Sigdo Koppers\uc2  (SK.SN) and other parties. CTI isn't listed on the Ipsa index.\par \par \uc2 Sidgo Koppers, which is on the Ipsa, rose 2.5% to CLP819.90.\par \par \uc2 In other local market news, the peso ended slightly stronger versus the dollar after fluctuating all session as institutional investors sold greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP467.45 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP468.10, after trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP469.05.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Monday at an average rate of CLP467.78 to the dollar. It has accumulated $8.05 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as fixed-income market participants weighed the possibility of another round of stimulus measures in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.57%, from 2.56% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.58%, from 2.55% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 08-22-11 1530ET ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110822e78m0004z\par }