\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Mildly Stronger On Institutional Dollar Sales\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 18:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso ended slightly stronger versus the dollar Monday after fluctuating all session as institutional investors sold greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP467.45 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP468.10, after trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP469.05.\par \par \uc2 Chile's private pension fund managers, the nation's largest institutional investors, "provided a steady stream of dollar sales during the day, boosting the peso slightly," said Flavio Magnasco, trader at bank Banco Falabella.\par \par \uc2 The peso, however, pared back part of its gains and as recently as 30 minutes before market close was in the red, as the euro slipped against the greenback and as U.S. markets lost their prior momentum.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of Chile's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The euro slipped against the dollar as investors pondered whether U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke could offer a new stimulus package at the annual gathering of central bankers in Jackson Hole, Wyo., on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "There is speculation that Bernanke could announce another round of quantitative easing on Friday," said Cristian Donoso, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  head at Banchile Inversiones.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Monday at an average rate of CLP467.78 to the dollar. It has accumulated $8.05 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as fixed-income market participants weighed the possibility of another round of stimulus measures in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.57%, from 2.56% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.58%, from 2.55% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 22-08-11 1648GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110822e78m00137\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
