\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Closes Stronger On Dollar Sales; Euro, Copper Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 20:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep rates pat, the Chilean peso ended stronger versus the dollar Friday as institutional investors sold dollars and international copper prices and the euro recovered.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP468.10 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of CLP469.95, after trading in a range of CLP467.60 to CLP470.00.\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held rates at 5.25% as expected, and removed the rate-tightening bias from its post-monetary-policy communique.\par \par \uc2 The peso, however, "firmed as institutional investors sold dollars before the weekend," said Sebastian Momberg, trader at bank BCI.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange rose 0.6% on the day to $8,820 a metric ton, supported by a weaker dollar and bargain hunting.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, as Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of Chile's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar sold off sharply against its major rivals, sending the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, with Europe's sovereign-debt woes far from over, the peso will likely weaken over the coming days to around CLP470, said BCI's Momberg.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Friday at an average rate of CLP468.93 to the dollar. It has accumulated $8 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's widely expected decision to hold rates steady.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.56%, from 2.57% on Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.55%, from 2.58% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 19-08-11 1809GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110819e78j0014e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
