\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.7% Lower On Global Growth, Euro Debt Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 22:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.7% lower Thursday, despite strong local economic-growth data, as disappointing U.S. economic data and renewed fears about Europe's sovereign debt troubles fueled risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa fell to 4156.45 points, paring back part of its earlier losses in the last minutes of trading. Market volume, meanwhile, slipped to 72.17 billion Chilean pesos ($153.6 million) from Wednesday's closing volume of CLP76.40 billion.\par \par \uc2 Although Chile's gross domestic product jumped 6.8% on the year in the second quarter, beating expectations of a 6.5% increase, the Ipsa slumped on the back of a grim mix of U.S. economic data and worries European banks will be walloped by the euro zone's debt woes.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. The DJIA ended 419.17 points, or 3.7% lower, at 10991.04.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, chemical and specialty fertilizer producer SQM's (SQM) more liquid B-series (SQM-B.SN) lost 2.7% to CLP27,850.00; bank \uc2 Banco Santander Chile\uc2  (SAN, BSANTANDE.SN) decreased 3.3% to CLP36.53; and power holding company \uc2 Enersis\uc2  (ENI, ENERSIS.SN) slipped 3.0% to CLP180.51.\par \par \uc2 Flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN), however, managed to post a 0.7% increase to CLP11,790.00 after the antitrust agency of Brazil's Justice Ministry approved LAN's proposed merger between with TAM (TAMM4.BR). The approval comes on the heels of a similar go-ahead from the Brazilian Finance ministry's antitrust agency, SEAE, last week.\par \par \uc2 The merger is still pending a ruling from Chile's TDLC antitrust court and from Brazil's top antitrust regulator Cade.\par \par \uc2 "This is obviously a good sign for the merger, with two out of three approvals in Brazil now out of the way. Now, the TDLC's ruling should ready in the coming weeks," said Pablo Alvarez, analyst at bank Banco Penta.\par \par \uc2 In other local market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar despite strong local growth data, as global growth fears pressured international copper prices and the euro lower.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP469.95 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP466.50, having traded in a range of CLP468.50 to CLP471.95.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP470.07 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.95 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as fixed-income participants kept their eyes on the volatile situation abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.57%, from 2.60% Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.58%, from 2.63% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 18-08-11 2017GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110818e78i0019f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
