\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Weaker As Copper, Euro Drop On Growth Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 19:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso ended weaker versus the dollar Thursday despite strong local growth data, as global growth fears pressured international copper prices and the euro lower.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP469.95 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP466.50, having traded in a range of CLP468.50 to CLP471.95.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that gross domestic product jumped 6.8% on the year in the second quarter, beating expectations of a 6.5% increase.\par \par \uc2 Despite the strong growth data, the peso fell as spot copper prices in London slumped 0.8% to $3.99297 a pound, according to Chilean state copper commission Cochilco, as a spate of disappointing U.S. economic data and continued worries about Europe's sovereign debt troubles fueled risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of Chile's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently dropped 1% against the dollar, helping to pressure the peso.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, global equities markets, which are often a bellwether of risk appetite, fell sharply, adding pressure to the peso's fall.\par \par \uc2 "The combination of negative factors all pulled the peso down...however, despite the pessimistic outlook the peso has consistently been unable to break support at CLP475, pointing to the fact that participants aren't crazy about holding too many dollars," said Andres Perez at Valores Security.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP470.07 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.95 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as fixed-income participants kept their eyes on the volatile situation abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.57%, from 2.60% Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.58%, from 2.63% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 18-08-11 1758GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110818e78i0018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
