\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Mildly Higher, Buoyed By Copec\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2011\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended mildly higher Wednesday, with fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) gaining as participants anticipated strong quarterly earnings.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended 0.2% higher at 4223.60 points, while market volume grew to 76.40 billion Chilean pesos ($163.8 million), compared with CLP66.23 billion on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 With pulp and wood products company CMPC (CMPC.SN) recently reporting a 36.5% on-year rise in second-quarter net profit to $166.9 million on the back of strong international wood pulp prices, participants expect Copec, the nation's biggest pulp producer, to also post a firm bottom line.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are awaiting Copec's earnings in the coming days and expecting good results," said Rodrigo Arriagada, analyst with brokerage Molina y Swett.\par \par \uc2 Copec, the heaviest weighted share on the Ipsa, rose 1.5% to CLP7,499.20.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa tracked the Dow Jones Industrial Average's prior strong surge. As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the DJIA.\par \par \uc2 Although the DJIA ended only 0.04% higher at 11410.21 points, it was up 124 points earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, power producer \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN) increased 1.7% to CLP786.93 and bank \uc2 CorpBanca\uc2  (BCA, CORPBANCA.SN) grew 1.7% to CLP6.90.\par \par \uc2 In other local market news, the peso ended 1.1% stronger against the dollar, with investors selling dollars as international copper prices rose and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened broadly.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP466.50 to the dollar, versus Tuesday's close of CLP471.80, while trading in a range of CLP466.40 to CLP469.55.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP468.40 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.90 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as institutional investors bought bonds.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.60%, from 2.61% Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.63%, from 2.67% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 17-08-11 2026GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110817e78h0016h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
