\par\par \b \uc2 Chilean Stocks Close Lower Amid Pessimism Over Global Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolina Pica   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2011\par 22:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended lower Tuesday amid investor pessimism regarding global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa fell 0.8% to 4215.44 points, erasing its early-session gains. Market volume, meanwhile, retreated to 66.23 billion Chilean pesos ($140.4 million), compared with CLP94.42 billion on Friday. All markets were closed Monday for a religious holiday.\par \par \uc2 Global growth concerns were fueled by anemic German gross domestic product data.\par \par \uc2 In addition, markets around the world were waiting for an announcement from European leaders proposed measures for the euro zone. But the measures disappointed as they didn't include the issuance of euro-zone bonds, traders said.\par \par \uc2 In early trading, Chilean stocks ventured into positive territory, but lost ground once the proposal from German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicolas Sarkozy was digested.\par \par \uc2 Commodities-related shares were hardest hit by the global growth pessimism as demand could take a downturn if activity slows down.\par \par \uc2 Among commodity-related shares, pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) slid 3% to CLP20,488.00 and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) tumbled 3.7% to CLP7,390.10.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the Chilean peso ended weaker against the dollar Tuesday, erasing midsession gains, also in reaction to European leaders' plans for the euro zone not meeting market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Tuesday at an average rate of CLP472.73 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.85 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP471.80 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP470.50, after trading in a range of CLP470.80 to CLP473.35.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed as the market awaits the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly monetary decision later in the week. Yields on shorter-term bonds fell, while those on longer-term bonds rose.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen holding the benchmark overnight lending rate steady at 5.25% when it meets Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, from 2.62% Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.67%, from 2.63% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8919; carolina.pica@dowjones.com [ 16-08-11 2028GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110816e78g00162\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
