\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Slightly Stronger As Investors Close Positions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 20:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso closed slightly stronger versus the dollar Friday, although it pared back most of its earlier gains, as participants closed positions before a long holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 Monday is a religious holiday, and Chilean markets, banks and government offices will be closed.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP470.50 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of CLP470.90. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a range of CLP467.70 to CLP471.80.\par \par \uc2 With volatility still reigning in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets, on fears about weak economic growth in the U.S. and pervasive euro-zone sovereign debt troubles, "participants opted to close positions ahead of the holiday weekend," said Osvaldo Molina, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at local brokerage and investment bank LarrainVial.\par \par \uc2 While international copper prices and the euro continue to rise and fall with the ebb and flow of market volatility, the peso will likely trade rangebound.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer, accounting for a third of global supply, and because a third of Chile's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often takes trading cues from copper's international prices and the euro's movements versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In the short term, the peso will likely trade in a range of CLP468-CLP475, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Friday at an average rate of CLP470.29 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.80 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower on decreasing private-sector inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 Private sector inflation expectations are retreating and moving toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target of 3%, plus or minus one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.62%, from 2.65% Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.63%, from 2.66% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 12-08-11 1814GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110812e78c001dt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
