\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Chile Peso Ends Marginally Weaker At Fresh Eight-Week Low\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2011\par 19:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 10 Aug 2011 13:54 EDT DJ  Chile Peso Ends Marginally Weaker At Fresh Eight-Week Low\par \par \uc2 By Anthony Esposito\par \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso closed marginally weaker versus the dollar Wednesday, at a fresh eight-week low, as the euro fell sharply against the greenback on concerns France may be the next triple-A-rated country to face a credit downgrade.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP473.20 to the dollar, versus Tuesday's close of CLP473.00, while trading in a range of CLP469.90 to CLP474.70.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled against the dollar on ongoing euro-zone sovereign debt concerns and fears that France's triple-A credit rating might be at risk.\par \par \uc2 Investors bought dollars locally "as the macro scenario turned negative again with fears about a double-dip recession in the U.S. being coupled with worries France may now be downgraded," a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, as Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange closed down 1.6% at $8,590 a metric ton tracking U.S. markets lower.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP472.46 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.70 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as fixed-income participants kept their eyes on the tricky scenario abroad and as local private pension fund managers bought bonds in a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheduled auction.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, from 2.64% Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.62%, from 2.70% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 10, 2011 13:54 ET (17:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110810e78a006vj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
