\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.6% Lower On US Double-Dip Recession Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 22:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--After swaying broadly higher and then lower, the Chilean blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.6% in the red, amid new fears about a double-dip recession in the U.S. and despite strong domestic economic-activity data.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended lower at 4076.98 points, but had fallen as much as 3.3% to 4007.87 points by midday, before paring back losses as investors cheered news that Italy will speed up its fiscal consolidation timetable. Volume fell to 138.02 billion Chilean pesos ($296.9 million), versus CLP148.33 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the opening bell, the Ipsa jumped as much as 1.1% on the back of stronger-than-expected domestic economic activity data and a better-than-anticipated reading on U.S. employment levels, following Thursday's massive 3.9% drop, the Ipsa's largest single-day slide since October 2008.\par \par \uc2 As enthusiasm for the U.S. employment data faded, the Ipsa swung around and headed lower, in line with the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the DJIA.\par \par \uc2 "We're witnessing a crisis of confidence on the back of fears about slowing growth in the U.S. or even the, as yet, remote possibility of a new recession. The U.S. accounts for nearly a fourth of the globe's gross domestic product, so a slowdown would affect everyone," said Ximena Garcia, head of studies with brokerage EuroAmerica.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) fell 2.1% to CLP11,400.00; power generator \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN) shed 1.6% to CLP761.00; and bank \uc2 Banco de Chile\uc2  (BCH, CHILE.SN) slipped 1.8% to CLP61.00.\par \par \uc2 Commodities-related shares also fell sharply, due to concerns that slowing global growth would hurt demand for the products they produce.\par \par \uc2 Fertilizer and specialty-chemical producer SQM's B shares (SQM, SQM-B.SN) lost 1.4% to CLP26,600.00; integrated steel and iron-ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) lost 2.7% to CLP19,110.00; pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) declined 1.4% to CLP20,511.00; and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) decreased 3.1% to CLP7,251.00.\par \par \uc2 The four commodities-related companies have a combined 27.6% weighting on the Ipsa.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended weaker against the dollar, due to intensifying fears about a double-dip U.S. recession and falling international copper prices.\par \par \uc2 The peso, however, cut its prior losses by about half after the euro surged versus the dollar on reports that Italy and the European Union agreed on reforms.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended at CLP464.90 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of CLP462.60, while trading in a range of CLP461.40 to CLP467.30.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Friday at an average rate of CLP457.10 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.55 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as fixed-income market participants kept abreast of developments abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.68%, from 2.66% Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.76%, from 2.75% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 05-08-11 2015GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110805e785001rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
