\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Loses 1% As Euro, Copper Drop On Global Growth Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 18:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso was trading 1.0% weaker against the dollar Thursday afternoon, tracking dropping international copper prices and a slumping euro on the back of mounting fears about global growth.\par \par \uc2 The peso was recently at CLP462.55 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP458.10, while trading in a range of CLP460.70 to CLP462.50.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for a third of global supply, the peso often takes trading cues from international copper prices.\par \par \uc2 Spot copper prices in London fell 1.0% to $4.28736 a pound, according to Chilean state copper commission Cochilco, on a broad dollar strength and brewing worries about economic growth in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell hard against the dollar, after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said that downside economic risks "may have intensified" and that recent data showed the growth pace in Europe has decelerated.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government, meant to stem the yen's strength, also rattled market sentiment and fueled a flight from risk.\par \par \uc2 "The only thing holding back the peso from falling even further, especially with international equity markets plummeting, is the fact that copper prices haven't fallen more sharply," said Sergio Tricio, studies head with trader \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP460.95 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.50 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 (Peso quote provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 --By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 04-08-11 1646GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784001ha\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
