\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Peso Closes Weaker As Copper Prices, Euro Slip\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 19:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended weaker versus the dollar Tuesday, as international copper prices slipped and as the euro fell against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP458.70 to the dollar, compared with Monday's close of CLP456.90. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a range of CLP457.00 to CLP459.70.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for a third of global supply, the peso often takes trading cues from the industrial metal's international prices.\par \par \uc2 Spot copper prices in London fell 2.0% to $4.36810 a pound, according to Chilean state copper commission Cochilco, on the back of global growth concerns, despite an ongoing strike at the \uc2 BHP Billiton Ltd\uc2 -controlled (BHP, BHP.AU) Escondida copper mine.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded lower versus the dollar as European debt concerns resurfaced and as a U.S. debt deal initially boosted the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainties surrounding the U.S. debt-deal have kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on edge in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone is nervous about the U.S. debt deal and whether ratings agencies will still decide to downgrade the U.S.; that's been injecting a lot of volatility into world \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks," said Francisca Roa, an analyst at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange research company Netgociando.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Tuesday at an average rate of CLP456.95 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.40 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower tracking the tumble in U.S. Treasurys ad investors continue to move into lower-risk assets.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.72%, from 2.74% on Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.84%, from 2.87% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 02-08-11 1738GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110802e7820019u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
