\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends At 3-Year High, Investors Watch US Debt Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2011\par 19:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended slightly stronger versus the dollar Thursday at a new three-year-high, as nervous investors kept a close watch of a contentious U.S. debt vote.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP456.30 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's close of CLP456.50. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in a range of CLP455.10 to CLP456.95.\par \par \uc2 Rising international copper prices helped prop up the peso, as three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange rose 0.4% to $9,814 per metric ton.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 "Markets around the world are looking at what's going on in the U.S., the uncertainty generated by that situation has seeped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equity markets globally," said Eduardo Kutscher, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader for local investment bank Celfin Capital.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, earlier in the day, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain said he was concerned the U.S. debt standoff could have repercussions on the peso.\par \par \uc2 A stronger peso makes Chilean exports, which represent about half of the country's gross domestic product, less competitive abroad.\par \par \uc2 "This problem originates in the U.S., but it's being felt around the world...and despite our strong economy we're still part of the global economy," Larrain said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP456.19 to the dollar. It has accumulated $7.25 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the fixed-income market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed in a light trading session.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.75%, from 2.74% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.89%, from 2.90% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Carolina Pica contributed to this article. [ 28-07-11 1750GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110728e77s001p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
