\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mixed, Brazil real slumps on new tax\b0\par\par\uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2011\par 22:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Derivatives tax knocks real from 12-year high\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso hits 3-year high, more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 * Real sheds 1.33 pct, Mexico's peso dips 0.16 pct\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, July 27 (Reuters) - Brazil's government surprised the market with new capital controls on Wednesday, knocking the real off a 12-year high, but the measures are unlikely to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from regaining its strength.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso hit a three-year high, raising concerns of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  there, while Mexico's peso weakened as investors grew increasingly nervous about the political stalemate over raising the U.S. debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government imposed a 1 percent tax on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, while allowing for a tax of up to 25 percent in the future. For details, see [ID:nN1E76Q01I]\par \par \uc2 Brazil's finance minister, Guido Mantega, said the government stood ready to take further actions to curb the real's strength, which is hurting domestic industries by curbing export profits and cheapening rival imports.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of people thought the government was not going to take further measures," said Roberto Padovani, a strategist at West LB in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that this same rule allows for further tax hikes means that risk in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has dramatically increased," he added.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> bid 1.33 percent weaker at 1.5585 per dollar, heading for its steepest one-day loss in nearly three months. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pared losses of nearly 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The new rules are the latest in a series of measures that Brazil has taken to discourage banks and speculators from gaming the global financial system in the so-called "carry trade," in which investors borrow low-interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and invest in higher-yielding ones.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the new measures would likely end up reducing the yield that investors can earn on non-deliverable forward contracts <BRLNDFOR=>, which is a favored vehicle for foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 RBS Securities strategist Flavia Cattan-Naslausky said the measures would reduce liquidity in the local market, which may increase the effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar purchases in taming the real's gains.\par \par \uc2 The market had shrugged off recent threats of new measures, pushing the real past 1.55 per dollar this week -- a level that many saw the government closely eyeing.\par \par \uc2 The country's capital controls and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy have occasionally spurred steep, short-term losses in the real. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained an appreciation trend since 2009 as investors are drawn to Brazil's double-digit interest rates and strong domestic growth.\par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, the new measures will not likely be very effective to stem the real's appreciation," said Igor Arsenin, head of Latin American fixed-income strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "You have foreign direct investment flows and you have the overall global dollar weakness. People will still be looking to acquire Brazilian assets, but this will make it more complicated," he said.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso <CLP=CL> firmed to a three-year high, bidding 0.24 percent stronger at 456.50 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso has strengthened despite a pause in interest rate hikes this month after a string of increases took the benchmark rate to 5.25 percent. The peso's gains have raised concerns that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step up its dollar purchases, or try some other measure.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso <MXN=> slipped 0.16 percent to 11.6574 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 If U.S. politicians fail to raise the debt ceiling, U.S. interest rates could rise sharply and crimp the economic recovery in the United States, Mexico's top trading partner.\par \par \uc2 If the debt limit is raised without spending cuts, ratings agencies could still downgrade the United States' debt rating, which could also push up interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Also hurting the Mexican peso, a report from the U.S. Federal Reserve showed economic growth slowed in much of the United States in June and early July, which bodes poorly for Mexican exports. [ID:nN1E76Q1G2]\par \par \uc2 "The Fed's report and the impasse in the U.S. debt talks are hurting the peso," said a trader in Mexico City. "If there is no deal by Friday, the peso could weaken to 11.85 or 11.90."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Dan Grebler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110730e77r000ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
