\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Lower As Pulp Producers, Retailers Slip\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2011\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended lower Tuesday, ignoring soaring U.S. markets, and instead pulled down by slumping pulp and paper producers and retailers.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa slipped 0.2% to 4662.09 points, while market volume grew to 78.58 billion Chilean pesos ($170.1 million), compared with CLP46.20 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. The DJIA ended 202.26 points, or 1.6% higher, at 12587.42 points, after President Barack Obama praised a $3.7 trillion deficit-reduction plan, saying the proposal represents a "very significant step" forward in deficit talks.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa, however, shrugged off surging U.S. markets as heavy-weighted pulp and paper producers slumped as international prices for wood pulp declined.\par \par \uc2 "Continued worries about sovereign debt problems in Europe, which is an important consumer of wood pulp, are hitting prices," said Andres Galarce, analyst with brokerage EuroAmerica.\par \par \uc2 Pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) fell 2.4% to CLP23,620.00 and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) lost 1.2% to CLP8,349.00.\par \par \uc2 Copec recently scratched plans for a $105 million plywood plant in southern Chile, "which is also helping to pull down its share price," said Galarce.\par \par \uc2 Also a commodities-related company, integrated steel and iron ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) declined 0.7% to CLP21,350.00.\par \par \uc2 Chemical and specialty fertilizer producer SQM's (SQM) more liquid B-series (SQM-B.SN), however, jumped 1.5% to CLP30,500.00 after local investment bank and brokerage LarrainVial gave the company a buy recommendation and a 12-to-18-month target price of CLP34,600.\par \par \uc2 Retailers were also among the biggest decliners.\par \par \uc2 Within the sector, retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) shed 1.4% to CLP3,200.00 after the company's chief financial officer stepped down.\par \par \uc2 Department store La Polar (LAPOLAR.SN), which is being investigated for alleged fraud, dropped 4.2% to CLP526.86.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso strengthened against the dollar as international copper prices rose and the euro firmed against the greenback on the back of strong U.S. housing data and optimism for a Greek financing deal.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP461.90 to the dollar, compared with CLP462.90 on Monday, while trading in a range of CLP460.70 to CLP462.75.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Tuesday at an average rate of CLP462.00 to the dollar, having accumulated $6.90 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed following a scheduled auction of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -issued sovereign debt.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.75%, off from 2.76% on Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged on the day at 2.89%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 19-07-11 2033GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110719e77j0017b\par }\page 