\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End Firmer, Govt Utilities Stakes Sale In Spotlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2011\par 22:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended higher Friday as the local market was focused on the government's sale of most of its stakes in the Essbio (ESSBIO-A.SN) and Esval (ESVAL-A.SN) water utilities.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa rose 0.6% to 4724.80 points, while market volume surged to 313.30 billion Chilean pesos ($677.6 million), as trading in Essbio and Esval accounted for 83% of volume. The prior session, market volume totaled CLP75.79 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Ontario Teachers' Pension Plan\uc2  bought all the shares in Essbio and Esval the government put out on the market, consolidating its majority stake in the utilities.\par \par \uc2 Esval's shares jumped 21.2% to CLP0.08 and Essbio slipped 10.7% to CLP15.18.\par \par \uc2 Also, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision, as expected, to keep the benchmark overnight rate steady at 5.25% at its monthly monetary policy meeting late Thursday helped lift the local market.\par \par \uc2 "The bank's decision to hold rates lent support to shares," said Camila Pindar, analyst with brokerage \uc2 CorpBanca\uc2  Corredores de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, expectations for Chinese demand for commodities to remain strong, boosted commodities-related companies.\par \par \uc2 RBS noted that Chinese copper demand should remain strong over the rest of the year, and could fuel higher prices.\par \par \uc2 Within the sector, integrated steel and iron ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) grew 0.7% to CLP22,254, fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) gained 0.6% to CLP8,643.50, and chemical and specialty fertilizer producer SQM's (SQM) more liquid B-series (SQM-B.SN) climbed 0.7% to CLP30,150.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy decision\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP462.40 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of CLP461.70, while trading in a range of CLP461.30 to CLP462.95.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its ongoing $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Friday at an average rate of CLP461.95 to the dollar, having accumulated $6.80 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed after the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept rates pat.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.74%, from 2.73% on Thursday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.89%, from 2.90% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 15-07-11 2038GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110715e77f0014y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
