\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End Lower On Global Growth Concerns, Pare Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2011\par 22:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended mildly lower Thursday, after paring back earlier losses, as health-care company \uc2 Cruz Blanca Salud\uc2 's (CRUZBLANCA.SN) initial public offering of shares grabbed market attention and as global growth concerns weighed.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended 0.3% lower at 4694.88 points, although it had fallen as much as 1% in earlier trading. Market volume, meanwhile, jumped to 218.2 billion Chilean pesos ($460.1 million), compared to CLP80.8 billion in the prior session, after \uc2 Cruz Blanca Salud\uc2  debuted on the Santiago Stock Exchange, listing 222.1 million shares at CLP500 each and raising $233.8 million.\par \par \uc2 In its first day of trading, the health-care company's shares lost 0.3% to CLP498.50.\par \par \uc2 Weak U.S. jobs data and ongoing worries about euro-zone debt sparked a new bout or risk aversion and helped to push global markets lower.\par \par \uc2 An unconfirmed report that European leaders had reached a breakthrough on a Greek austerity plan, however, helped push U.S. markets, and the Ipsa with it, higher.\par \par \uc2 As several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. The DJIA erased most of its earlier losses, ending 0.5% lower at 12050 points.\par \par \uc2 "The Ipsa tracked U.S. markets very closely today, slumping in the morning and then recovered in late trading in line with rebounding U.S. markets," said analyst Christopher Baillarie at local investment bank BCI.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, bank \uc2 Banco Santander Chile\uc2  (SAN, BSANTANDE.SN) lost 0.9% to CLP40.90; power generator \uc2 Endesa\uc2  (EOC, ENDESA.SN) fell 0.3% to CLP876.50; and beverage company CCU (CCU, CCU.SN) shed 0.5% to CLP5,380.00.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, financially distressed La Polar, whose shareholders approved a $200 million capital increase the prior session and likely kept the company from going bankrupt, leapt 17.0% to CLP632.00.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, La Polar disclosed irregularities in its in-store credit-card division and said it will have to set aside almost $900 million to cover bad loans to its customers.\par \par \uc2 In other local market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar, as global growth concerns seized markets, dragging the euro lower against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushing international copper prices below $9,000 a metric ton.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP474.20 to the dollar, compared with CLP472.20 the prior session, while trading in a tight range of CLP474.20 to CLP475.30.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP475.00 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $6.05 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as fixed-income market participants kept a watchful eye on the stream of negative headlines coming from abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.62%, from 2.61% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged on the day at 2.90%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-7