\par\par \b \uc2 Chile's Peso Closes Weaker As Dollar Firms On Fed Statement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2011\par 20:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended weaker versus the dollar Wednesday, as the greenback firmed against the euro after the Federal Reserve said it will continue with plans to end its $600 billion bond-buying program on June 30 as planned.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP472.20 to the dollar, compared with CLP471.40 the prior session, while trading in a tight range of CLP471.50 to CLCP472.60.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are shipped to European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, investors are now looking beyond the vote of confidence the Greek Prime Minister won on Tuesday and to the difficult task of passing tough austerity measures in the Mediterranean nation, sapping some of the euro's strength.\par \par \uc2 "That will be the complicated part, getting those measures to pass," said Flavio Magnasco, a trader with bank Banco Falabella.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the peso slipped as institutional investors bought dollars.\par \par \uc2 "Towards the end of the trading session, investors closed their intraday positions and bought dollars," said Raul Lopez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with investment bank Banco Penta.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP472.38 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $6 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed as fixed-income market participants digested the Federal Reserve's statement.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, from 2.60% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.90%, from 2.92% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 22-06-11 1804GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110622e76m0017d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
