\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.7% Lower On Retail Woes, Global Growth Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2011\par 22:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.7% lower Wednesday as department store La Polar's (LAPOLAR.SN) growing financial and legal problems sucked the wind out the retail sector and as global growth worries dragged international markets lower.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa dropped to 4658.40 points, while volume surged to 615.5 billion Chilean pesos ($1.31 billion) after the government auctioned off a 30% stake in water utility \uc2 Aguas Andinas SA\uc2  (AGUAS-A.SN) for $987 million early in the session. Market volume totaled CLP77.7 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Aguas Andinas lost 4.6% to CLP248.00.\par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the local SVS securities regulator decided to suspend trading of La Polar shares until June 17 after the retailer last week had disclosed it will need to provision an estimated $430 million to cover its bad-credit portfolio, after detecting what the company called "unauthorized practices."\par \par \uc2 The national prosecutor's office opened a criminal investigation in the matter, while the Lower House, the SBIF banking and financial institutions regulator, and the government, through the Finance Ministry, are all conducting their own investigations into La Polar's unsavory accounting practices.\par \par \uc2 "There are two big fears here. First, that other retailers also cooked their books, which is something that seems unlikely," said Marcelo Barrero, analyst with brokerage EuroAmerica.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, investors are concerned that the government's investigation into the matter will usher in tighter regulations for retailers that issue credit cards.\par \par \uc2 "That's something that's more likely to happen," said Barrero.\par \par \uc2 Within the sector, retailer Hites (HITES.SN) plummeted 6.2% to CLP560.00, department store Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) lost 3.1% to CLP559.00, retail holding \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) shed 3.5% to CLP3,250.00 and department store Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) dropped 3.1% to CLP4,720.00.\par \par \uc2 Also, as several of the Ipsa's heavier-weighted shares also trade in New York, the Ipsa often tracks the Dow Jones Industrial Average. In recent trading, the DJIA ended 1.5% lower as a bad mix of economic news in the U.S. and fears of contagion around a Greek default weighed on investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Among dually listed shares, flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN) lost 2.1% to CLP12,462.00. In recent days, the airline has needed to cancel or delay many flights to and from Argentina and, to a lesser degree, Australia and New Zealand because of the presence of volcanic ash from the Puyehue volcano in southern Chile.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar, as the euro slipped to a one-month low against the greenback on increasing concerns about the impact of possible Greek debt default.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at CLP468.60 to the dollar, compared with Tuesday's close at CLP465.90, after trading in a tight range of CLP467.10 to CLP469.15.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP467.21 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.75 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended lower as fixed-income participants kept a wary eye on falling global stock markets a slipping euro and as retailer \uc2 Cencosud SA\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) issued $115.5 million in a 20-year, peso-denominated bond.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.62%, from 2.63% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.91%, from 2.93% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 15-06-11 2015GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110615e76f0017c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
