\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End Lower, As La Polar Saps Confidence In Retail\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2011\par 22:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended lower Tuesday, as department store La Polar's (LAPOLAR.SN) financial woes and growing legal troubles sapped confidence in the retail sector.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa ended 0.4% lower at 4646.13 points, while volume grew to 77.7 billion Chilean pesos ($166.8 million) from CLP70.7 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the local SVS securities regulator decided to suspend trading of La Polar shares until June 17 after the retailer, last week, disclosed it will need to provision an estimated $430 million to cover its bad-credit portfolio, after detecting what the company called "unauthorized practices."\par \par \uc2 La Polar's deepening problems, which include a criminal investigation by the national prosecutor's office, an investigation by the SBIF banking and financial institutions regulator and an investigation by the government, have stirred concern about the retail sector and the likelihood of tougher regulations for credit-card issuers.\par \par \uc2 "The whole situation has generated a feeling of unease about the retail sector," said Aflredo Ugarte, analyst with brokerage \uc2 CorpBanca\uc2  Corredores de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 Retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) shed 1.5% to CLP3,366.40; retailer Hites (HITES.SN) dropped 4.8% to CLP597.09; rival Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) lost 0.6% to CLP576.89; and the nation's largest department-store operator Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) fell 0.2% to CLP4,872.10.\par \par \uc2 Mall operator and developer Parque Arauco (PARAUCO.SN) declined 1.7% to CLP1,100.00.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended at a two-week high versus the dollar, as strong Chinese industrial production data boosted international copper prices.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at CLP465.90 to the dollar, compared with Monday's close at CLP467.60, after trading in a narrow range of CLP465.90 to CLP466.70.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Tuesday at an average rate of CLP466.49 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.7 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as global risk appetite increased and as fixed-income participants expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase rates by 25 basis points after market close.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release its post-meeting communique after 2200 GMT Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.63%, from 2.62% on Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.93%, from 2.91% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 14-06-11 2033GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110614e76e0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
