\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Closes At Two-Week High As China Data Boosts Copper\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2011\par 20:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended at a two-week high versus the dollar Tuesday, as strong Chinese industrial production data boosted international copper prices.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at CLP465.90 to the dollar, compared with Monday's close at CLP467.60, after trading in a narrow range of CLP465.90 to CLP466.70.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 In London trading, three-month copper ended 2.9% higher on the day at $9,169 a metric ton as investors focused on top metals consumer China's encouraging industrial production figures and shrugged off the Asian nation's less-than-aggressive monetary tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 "Today's economic data dispelled concerns about a slowdown of China's economy, fueling copper prices and helping to boost global stock markets," said Francisca Roa, analyst with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker Netgociando.\par \par \uc2 Also, U.S. stocks rose sharply, buttressing the peso, as U.S. retail sales fell less than expected and were encouraged by the strong industrial production report from China.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, which was recently up 1.2%, is often looked at to measure risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Tuesday at an average rate of CLP466.49 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.70 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended higher as global risk appetite increased and as fixed-income participants expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase rates by 25 basis points after market close.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release its post-meeting communique after 2200 GMT Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.63%, from 2.62% on Monday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.93%, from 2.91% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 14-06-11 1803GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110614e76e0017e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
