\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.2% Lower As Investors Jump Ship On La Polar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2011\par 22:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.2% lower Thursday as investors sold off shares in department store La Polar (LAPOLAR.SN) following the disclosure of the company's financial woes.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa closed lower at 4762.32 points, while market volume jumped to 145.8 billion Chilean pesos ($312.7 million) from CLP57.6 billion in the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Trading in La Polar and farmed-salmon producer and exporter \uc2 Australis Seafoods\uc2  (AUSTRALIS.SN), which debuted on the Santiago Stock Exchange Thursday and raised $71 million through the sale of a 12.8% equity stake, accounted for over 57% of market volume.\par \par \uc2 Australis Seafoods\uc2  ended 2.2% higher on its first day of trading at CLP189.00.\par \par \uc2 La Polar unexpectedly disclosed in a filing to the local SVS regulator it would provision up to CLP200 billion to cover its bad credit portfolio and would restructure management. Its shares dropped 42.4% to CLP1,344.90.\par \par \uc2 "Having lost so much of its market value, the company could be a potential acquisition target now...At this point, though, you don't really know where La Polar stands and it could even possibly go under," said Peter McMenamin, analyst with local investment bank Banchile Inversiones.\par \par \uc2 The momentum produced by La Polar's sell-off also pulled other retailers lower. Chile's largest department store Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) lost 1.0% to CLP5,089.40 and rival Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) also declined 1.0% to CLP624.03.\par \par \uc2 Also declining, fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) shed 2.0% to CLP8,587.70 and pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) decreased 1.5% to CLP23,960.00, giving up their strong gains the prior session.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended stronger versus the dollar, as international copper prices rose and institutional investors sold greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 The peso finished stronger at CLP466.30 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's close at CLP467.20, after trading in a range of CLP466.30 to CLP468.60.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP467.60 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.55 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed in thin over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.62%, from 2.61% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.91%, from 2.92%the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 09-06-11 2021GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110609e7690019b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
