\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Ends Weaker As Fed Outlook Boost Safe-Haven Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2011\par 20:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso ended weaker versus the dollar Wednesday, as Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's downbeat assessment of the U.S. economy late Tuesday sparked risk aversion, boosted the greenback and pulled international copper prices lower.\par \par \uc2 The peso finished at CLP467.20 to the dollar, compared with Tuesday's close at CLP466.20, after trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP468.50.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are bound for Europe, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against the dollar, which is viewed as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it continued to be torn between Greece's debt troubles and expectations of a lift in euro-zone interest rates.\par \par \uc2 As Chile is the world's premier copper producer, accounting for a third of the global supply, the peso also often takes trading cues from the industrial metal's international prices.\par \par \uc2 Following Bernanke's comments, three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange closed 1.2% lower at $9,030 per ton.\par \par \uc2 Chile's consumer price index, however, which increased more than expected in May -- a 0.4% increase from the previous month, versus a 0.3% expected gain-- helped to pare some of the peso's losses.\par \par \uc2 "The higher-than-expected CPI data support continued rate hikes, rather than the pauses the market had started to expect. That supports a stronger peso," said Andres de la Cerda, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  head at local investment bank Bice Inversiones.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP467.11 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.50 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed following the release of the May CPI data early in the session.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, from 2.62% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.92%, from 2.90% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 06-08-11 1415ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110608e76800073\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
