\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Slightly Higher, Buoyed By Pulp Producers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2011\par 22:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended slightly higher Wednesday, kept afloat by heavy-weighted pulp and paper producers, which jumped on increasing international wood pulp prices.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa closed 0.1% higher at 4819.39 points, while market volume fell to 57.6 billion Chilean pesos ($123.2 million) from CLP92.8 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) and pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN), which have a combined 16.1% weighting on the Ipsa, rose on the back of increasing international wood pulp prices and expectations for strong Chinese demand.\par \par \uc2 Copec gained 1.7% to CLP8,758.40 and CMPC climbed 1.3% to CLP24,319.00.\par \par \uc2 Also gaining, retailer Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) added 1.9% to CLP630.00, as investors viewed it as an attractive buying opportunity following recent steep losses.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's downbeat outlook for the U.S. economy late Tuesday, and higher-than-expected local consumer price index data, however, kept the Ipsa from rising further.\par \par \uc2 "Bernanke's comments reverberated through markets world-wide as everyone wonders where the U.S. economy is headed," said Andres Galarce, analyst with local brokerage EuroAmerica.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar, as Bernanke's assessment of the U.S. economy sparked risk aversion, boosted the greenback and pulled international copper prices lower.\par \par \uc2 The peso finished at CLP467.20 to the dollar, compared with Tuesday's close at CLP466.20, after trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP468.50.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Wednesday at an average rate of CLP467.11 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.50 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed following the release of the May CPI data early in the session.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, from 2.62% on Tuesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.92%, from 2.90% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 08-06-11 2034GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110608e768001dh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
