\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End 1.3% Lower As Peru's Humala Win Causes Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2011\par 22:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended 1.3% lower Monday, after left-wing nationalist Ollanta Humala was elected to be neighboring Peru's next president, sparking concerns about cross-border investments.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa closed lower, at 4814.09 points, while market volume fell to 65 billion Chilean pesos ($138.8 million) from CLP95.3 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Market confidence was shaken as investors worried that Chilean companies, including heavy-weighted retailers and flagship carrier \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2  (LFL, LAN.SN), could freeze their investment plans in Peru as they wait for concrete policies from Humala and cabinet members to be selected. In his prior presidential campaign in 2006, Humala espoused anti-Chilean policies.\par \par \uc2 "Since markets were to a large extent pricing a [Keiko] Fujimori victory, we believe a sell-off in financial assets--at least initially--is the expected scenario as investors question Humala's credibility in matters related to state involvement in the economy," said local investment bank Celfin Capital.\par \par \uc2 Humala is, however, signaling a "policy of moderation," Celfin Capital added.\par \par \uc2 Among Chilean companies with significant exposure to Peru, LAN fell 2.7%, to CLP13,140.00, retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) lost 2.4%, to CLP3,480.00, department store Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) declined 2.3%, to CLP5,160.00 and retailer Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) shed 3.1%, to CLP617.10.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended slightly weaker against the dollar on lower-than-expected economic growth data and the dollar's gains versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended at CLP468.20 to the dollar, compared with Friday's close at CLP467.70, after trading in a range of CLP467.50 to CLP469.00.\par \par \uc2 As part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $50 million Monday at an average rate of CLP467.80 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.4 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed, as participants geared up for state-owned bank BancoEstado's and shopping center operator Mall Plaza's bond issue scheduled for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended unchanged, at 2.60%, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.90%, up from 2.88% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 06-06-11 2114GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110606e7660010j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
