\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks End Higher; Retailers Up On Economic Growth Forecasts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2011\par 22:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended sharply higher Thursday, as retailers gained on forecasts for robust economic growth and domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa closed 0.8% higher at 4840.26 points, while market volume grew to 106.3 billion Chilean pesos ($226.6 million), from CLP101.9 billion the prior session.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, Finance Minister Felipe Larrain reaffirmed the government's 2011 gross domestic product growth outlook of "over 6%." Larrain's comments come on the heels of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development's projections for Chile's GDP to grow 6.5% this year and domestic demand to expand 9%.\par \par \uc2 "Forecasts for continued strong growth and demand are boosting retailers' share prices," said Andres Galarce, analyst with local brokerage EuroAmerica.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile's larger retailers also have \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Peru, their share prices have benefited from opinion polls showing conservative Congresswoman Keiko Fujimori ahead of left-wing nationalist Ollanta Humala before the June 5 runoff for president in Peru.\par \par \uc2 "It's not that the business community is thrilled with Fujimori, but she is definitely seen as the lesser of two evils. That has also helped to fuel local retailers," Galarce said.\par \par \uc2 Among retailers with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Peru, the nation's largest department store Falabella (FALABELLA.SN) gained 1.5% to CLP5,219.00, retail holding giant \uc2 Cencosud\uc2  (CENCOSUD.SN) expanded 1.9% to CLP3,600.00, and Ripley (RIPLEY.SN) rose 2.7% to CLP656.50.\par \par \uc2 Also gaining, fertilizer and specialty-chemical producer SQM's (SQM) more liquid B-series shares (SQM-B.SN) jumped 1.1% to CLP28,118.00, after local investment bank Banchile Inversiones gave the company a new target price of CLP34,000 per share.\par \par \uc2 SQM's surge helped boost other commodities-related companies.\par \par \uc2 Among them, integrated steel and iron-ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) increased 0.8% to CLP22,665.00, and fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) climbed 1.2% to CLP8,716.00. Copec's wood-pulp and forestry unit \uc2 Celulosa Arauco y Constitucion\uc2  posted a nearly threefold jump in first-quarter net profit to $172.5 million the prior session.\par \par \uc2 In other local markets, the peso ended stronger versus the dollar, after a lackluster session where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shrugged off most of the cues it usually takes direction from. The peso strengthened to CLP469.20 to the dollar, versus Wednesday's close of CLP471.40, after trading in a narrow range of CLP469.05 to CLP470.80.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Thursday at an average rate of CLP469.99. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $5.05 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed as market attention was focused on a corporate bond issue.\par \par \uc2 Local financing services provider Forum issued $21 million in 2.5-year bonds at a yield of 7%, which pushed shorter-term yields higher.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.64%, from 2.63% on Wednesday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.91%, from 2.92% the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Carolina Pica contributed to this article. [ 26-05-11 2032GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110526e75q001dr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
