\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Chile April industry seen +9.4 pct on quake rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2011\par 21:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WHAT: Chile industrial output <CLIP=ECI> for April\par \par \uc2 WHEN: Monday, May 30 at 9 a.m. (1300 GMT)\par \par \uc2 REUTERS FORECAST: Industrial output is seen rising 9.4 percent in April from a year earlier, according to the median forecast of 10 analysts and economists surveyed, with estimates for the projected increase ranging from 8 to 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Output rose 30.9 percent in March from a year earlier, the highest increase on record, given a low base of comparison in March 2010 following a devastating earthquake. Output rose a seasonally adjusted 2.4 percent compared to February. For more see [ID:nN28303503].\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH: The expected surge is due to the weak base of comparison after industry output fell in March and April last year following the Feb. 27 earthquake that hammered key industries. Output fell 1.3 percent in April 2010 from a year earlier after sinking 17.5 percent the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Strong domestic demand continues to fuel economic growth, though the pace of expansion has slowed.\par \par \uc2 Economic activity rose a seasonally adjusted 0.3 percent in March compared with February, easing from 0.4 percent growth in February, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed this month. [ID:nN05274343]\par \par \uc2 On a year-on-year basis <CLIP=ECI>, the IMACEC indicator soared 15.2 percent in March, accelerating from February's 7.2 percent year-on-year increase.\par \par \uc2 MARKET IMPACT: Strong industrial data could stoke the outlook for inflation, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says private inflation expectations have eased. Last week it said the pace of interest rate hikes may slow or even pause.\par \par \uc2 The bank is seen raising its benchmark rate 25 basis points to 5.25 percent at its June monetary policy meeting, slowing the pace after three straight aggressive 50-basis-point hikes, a fortnightly poll of traders showed on Wednesday. [ID:nN25110596]\par \par \uc2 Fresh pressure on inflation expectations would reinforce expectations for a 25-basis-point increase in June, and would add upward pressure on the peso <CLP=CL>, which is trading near three-year highs after erasing the initial impact of a $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program for 2011.\par \par \uc2 (For \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's statistics agency website for industrial production data, see: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ine.cl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.ine.cl}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 (For all Chile economic data, double-click: <ECONALLCL>)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Maria Jose Latorre; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 CHILE/INDUSTRIALOUTPUT (PREVIEW)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110526e75q001ly\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
