\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Peso Closes 1.4% Weaker On Tumbling Euro, Copper Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2011\par 19:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended 1.4% weaker versus the dollar Monday, tracking the euro's tumble after a slew of ratings agency warnings fueled fears about euro-zone debt and following sliding international copper prices.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened to CLP473.00 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP466.60, while trading in a range of CLP471.65 to CLP473.90.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the nation's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped versus the dollar after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  cut its outlook on Italy's credit rating to negative from stable over the weekend and Fitch Ratings revised Belgium's rating outlook to negative from stable, while affirming its rating at AA-plus.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, accounting for a third of global supply, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened on the back of falling international copper prices. Copper for three-month delivery closed 3.1% lower on the London Metal Exchange to $8,793 a metric ton, on weaker-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data and a surge in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "With this scenario of renewed euro-zone debt jitters and falling copper prices, we could see the peso weaken to CLP480 in the next couple of days," said a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, who asked not to be named.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million Monday at an average rate of CLP472.32. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.90 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended marginally lower as fixed-income market participants kept their eyes on the volatile scenario abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.61%, versus 2.62% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed at 2.88%, from 2.89% the prior session.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 23-05-11 1753GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110523e75n0014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
