\par\par \b \uc2 Chile Stocks Close Higher On Commodities-Related Companies\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2011\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's blue-chip Ipsa index ended slightly higher Monday, as commodities-related shares gained in line with recovering international copper prices, and as participants awaited first-quarter gross domestic product data later this week.\par \par \uc2 The Ipsa increased 0.1% to 4855.00 points, while market volume fell to 75.0 billion Chilean pesos ($159.1 million) from CLP77.3 billion the previous session.\par \par \uc2 Commodities-related companies, which have a heavy weighting on the Ipsa, as international copper prices gained on the back of a weak dollar. Although none of the commodities companies listed on the Ipsa are copper producers, their shares often rise and fall through association from copper's price variations since Chile is the world's premier producer.\par \par \uc2 Fuel and forestry conglomerate Copec (COPEC.SN) climbed 1.2% to CLP9,100.00; pulp and paper producer CMPC (CMPC.SN) increased 0.9% to CLP25,200.00; and integrated steel and iron ore producer Cap (CAP.SN) gained 1.0% to CLP23,150.00. The three companies have a nearly 22% weighting on the Ipsa.\par \par \uc2 Most sectors, however, traded flat as investors "await GDP data that will be released on Wednesday...they also waiting for the remaining big companies that still need to report their quarterly earnings, such as Copec," according to a local equity analyst.\par \par \uc2 In other market news, the peso ended weaker versus the dollar, despite a rebounding euro and recovering international copper prices, as institutional investors snapped up greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened to CLP471.30 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP467.40, while trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP471.70.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million on Monday at an average rate of CLP468.20. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.65 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed in light over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.64%, from 2.62% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged at 2.89%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 16-05-11 2026GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5057   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110516e75g0018n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
