\par\par \b \uc2 Chilean Peso Closes Weaker On Institutional Dollar Buying\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Esposito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2011\par 19:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso ended weaker versus the dollar Monday, despite a rebounding euro and recovering international copper prices, as institutional investors snapped up greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened to CLP471.30 to the dollar, versus Friday's close of CLP467.40, while trading in a range of CLP466.60 to CLP471.70.\par \par \uc2 As Chile's economy is highly export-dependent and nearly a third of the Andean nation's exports are bound for European ports, the peso often moves in the same direction as the euro does against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Also, because Chile is the world's largest copper producer and exporter, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants usually keep an eye on New York and London copper prices to gauge export revenue inflows and the general health of the Chilean economy.\par \par \uc2 The peso, however, shrugged off the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar, to its strongest level since late April and international copper price's recovery following an earlier slump.\par \par \uc2 "Fundamentals should have fueled a stronger peso, but institutional dollar buying pulled the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the other direction," said Sebastian Momberg, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with bank BCI.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bigger-than-expected 50-basis-points rate increase late Thursday, "should have also pushed the peso higher, but at current levels, dollar buying is attractive," said Momberg.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of its $12 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, purchased $50 million on Monday at an average rate of CLP468.20. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated $4.65 billion so far this year.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yields on inflation-indexed Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, or BCUs, ended mixed in light over-the-counter trading.\par \par \uc2 The yield on five-year BCU bonds ended at 2.64%, from 2.62% on Friday, while the yield on 10-year BCUs closed unchanged at 2.89%.\par \par \uc2 (Peso and bond quotes provided by Valor Futuro newswire.)\par \par \uc2 -By Anthony Esposito, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-715-8929; anthony.esposito@dowjones.com [ 16-05-11 1756GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110516e75g0017a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
